WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.880
Your eyes work in starlight and in sunshine.

2
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:11.020
A photoreceptor manages that by turning its own gain down as the background

3
00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:13.620
brightens. Calcium is the signal.

4
00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:16.540
Light lowers calcium in the outer segment.

5
00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:22.620
Falling calcium speeds the making of cGMP, and lets the active pigment be shut off

6
00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:27.680
sooner. That feedback keeps the cell answering to changes in light, instead of

7
00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:28.680
saturating.

8
00:00:29.233 --> 00:00:34.233
Light closes channels in the outer segment, so calcium entry falls, while a pump

9
00:00:34.233 --> 00:00:35.553
keeps removing calcium.

10
00:00:36.413 --> 00:00:41.793
At low calcium, the GCAPs speed cGMP synthesis, which reopens channels.

11
00:00:42.833 --> 00:00:47.973
Recoverin lets go of GRK1, the kinase that switches off activated pigment.

12
00:00:48.933 --> 00:00:52.473
Both push the cell back toward its dark state while the light stays on.

13
00:00:52.473 --> 00:00:54.553
That push is gain control.

14
00:00:55.583 --> 00:00:57.103
To test a loop, cut it.

15
00:00:57.863 --> 00:01:04.183
In mouse rods lacking both GCAPs, responses recover slowly, single-photon responses

16
00:01:04.183 --> 00:01:09.523
grow about five times larger, and adaptation to background light is badly impaired.

17
00:01:10.623 --> 00:01:12.743
Cones without GCAPs look much the same.

18
00:01:13.523 --> 00:01:16.603
So the GCAPs normally curb and shorten each response.

19
00:01:17.543 --> 00:01:22.763
In people, GCAP1 mutations are linked to severe inherited blinding disease.

20
00:01:23.867 --> 00:01:28.607
Sensitivity falls as the background brightens, and two numbers describe the fall.

21
00:01:29.587 --> 00:01:33.567
I0 is the background that halves sensitivity, and n sets the slope.

22
00:01:34.687 --> 00:01:39.667
Mouse cones lacking the GCAPs and recoverin reach half sensitivity at about a

23
00:01:39.667 --> 00:01:43.167
quarter of the control background, and then fall more steeply.

24
00:01:43.967 --> 00:01:47.867
Some adaptation remains even so, and what supplies it is still open.

25
00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:51.150
Rods and cones split the range of light.

26
00:01:52.010 --> 00:01:53.870
A rod can respond to a single photon.

27
00:01:54.910 --> 00:01:58.950
Cones are less sensitive, but they end each response faster, which suits bright

28
00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:01.950
light. Mouse recordings revise one rule.

29
00:02:02.770 --> 00:02:07.410
On a bright background, rod-driven responses dip, then recover with continued

30
00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:12.050
exposure. So in these mice, rods do not simply switch off in daylight.

31
00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:15.540
When photoreceptors die, the order matters.

32
00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:21.960
In typical retinitis pigmentosa, rods die first and cones die after, even when the

33
00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:23.960
faulty gene is not expressed in cones.

34
00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:27.420
Losing cones costs daylight and central vision.

35
00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:30.800
One candidate reason is lost rod support.

36
00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:37.560
A rod-derived factor, RdCVF, raises cone glucose uptake in experimental models.

37
00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:42.500
Oxidative damage is another candidate, and no single cause is settled.

38
00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:47.190
Gene therapy can now improve vision in some genetic forms.

39
00:02:48.070 --> 00:02:54.590
In a randomized trial, RPE65 gene augmentation improved navigation at low light

40
00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:56.730
levels over a control group at one year.

41
00:02:57.570 --> 00:03:03.870
An early editing study for CEP290 reported safety and a possible benefit, without a

42
00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:07.610
control group. The design decides what each study can claim.
